# Every known problem with the ML track, in view. Rule (inherited from quod,
# 2026-09-06): a gap that is known and unlisted is a defect of this ledger,
# not of the gap.
# status: open | fix-pending (code written, run not yet recorded) | closed <date> <how>
# severity: high = a cited metric can be wrong or unreproducible; medium = a
#           capability gap or a failed stage gate; low = cost, ergonomics, or
#           a by-design limit worth stating.
- id: L-1
  severity: medium
  status: open
  title: S3 gloss tower is representation-limited; same-module hard-drift AUC 0.807 < 0.90 gate
  detail: >
    Two S3 runs failed the gate on held-out modules. v1 (s3-gloss-20260910-174141,
    eval ca34a4b7...): hard AUC 0.767, retrieval MRR 0.398. v2 with module-grouped
    hard-negative batches and val early stopping (s3-gloss-20260911-012141, eval
    3d8a926c...): hard AUC 0.807, retrieval MRR 0.400 — mining gave +0.04 but the
    val probe saturated at ~0.84 and retrieval did not move at all. The statement
    tower is strong (S2 same-lock MRR 0.992); the gloss tower is the limit: English
    docstrings are byte-fallbacked by the Lean-only tokenizer (CID 3b33be81...) and
    the shared encoder was MLM-pretrained on Lean statements only. This is a
    representation deficit, not a tuning gap. The model remains useful for gross
    prose/statement mismatch (easy-drift AUC 0.977; 93.7% of matched pairs clear
    the 90th percentile of random mismatches), which is the practical quod Q-5 case.
  fix: >
    Either (a) a gloss-capable tokenizer trained on docstrings+statements and an
    MLM pretraining pass over both (a NEW tokenizer pin — changes the corpus
    contract for every downstream stage, so do it deliberately, not mid-stream), or
    (b) a pinned external prose encoder for the gloss tower with its checkpoint
    content-addressed — a doctrine decision (the plan excluded external artifacts).
    Decided 2026-09-11: record and move on; revisit with (a) when a tokenizer
    re-pin is planned.
- id: L-2
  severity: medium
  status: open
  title: S3's exact drift eval (docstring x gate-verified mutant) is deferred
  detail: >
    The plan's S3 criterion pairs a docstring with a gate-verified MUTANT of its
    statement. That corpus (Phase 3: mutants.jsonl with gate verdicts) is not
    built, so evaluate_s3.py gates on the strongest mutant-free proxy — same-module
    hard negatives — and records the exact test as deferred in every eval record.
  fix: build the Phase-3 mutant corpus (see L-3), then add the mutant-pair AUC to
    evaluate_s3.py as the primary gate.
- id: L-3
  severity: medium
  status: closed 2026-09-11 by quod corpus mutants-20260911 (manifest CID 06622cff...7f57), 19,007 mutants over 5,410 sampled theorems, Phase-3a labels (elaborates, lock_changed, operator; proof-side verdicts by construction). Statement-mutant gate_verdict remains Phase-3b (see L-2).
  title: S4 (mutant verdict predictor) is blocked on the Phase-3 mutant corpus
  detail: >
    No mutants.jsonl exists. It requires an Expr-level mutation walker in quod
    (binder-level hypothesis deletion, curated constant swaps, sorry/axiom
    injection) with per-mutant gate labels (lock_changed -> elaborates ->
    gate_verdict). CPU/Lean-bound; parallelizable with GPU training. Also
    unblocks L-2.
- id: L-5
  severity: medium
  status: open
  title: S5 proposals ranked by raw cosine are dominated by boilerplate near-duplicates
  detail: >
    S5 passed its gate (s5-equiv-20260911-125959, eval 1a0ed42c...: known-Iff
    MRR 0.564 >= 0.5, dedup 0.974, permutation 0.496) and emitted 100 novel
    proposals (CID f865e4ea...). But the top of that list is score-1.000 pairs
    inside one namespace (Algebra.Generators.Hom.*: congr_simp / equivAlgHom_*
    / comp_id) — auto-generated lemma variants with near-identical readable
    forms, not non-obvious equivalences. Submitting these to the gates would
    mostly confirm trivialities. The plan's real test (>= 10 of top 100
    discharged) is only meaningful on a list that excludes such pairs.
  fix: before gate submission, rerank proposals with a lexical-overlap penalty
    (e.g. token-Jaccard of the readable forms) and a same-namespace / same-
    module penalty, and require distinct locks whose canonical forms differ
    beyond binder names; then re-emit and submit. Gate submission itself needs
    a prover-in-the-loop (the gates verify a given proof; they do not find one)
    — a separate stage to design.
    2026-09-11 (quod Q-25) — the pool itself is wrong, not just its order;
    every proposal pairs two THEOREMS, and two proven statements are always
    equivalent as propositions, so attempting `A ↔ B` at the gates is vacuous.
    The rerank is deferred until the proposal generator draws from
    propositions whose truth is not already established (Iff sides
    `iff_lhs`/`iff_rhs` in the corpus, Prop-valued definitions) under shared
    binders; that is a doctrine change awaiting the user's call.
- id: L-6
  severity: medium
  status: open
  title: S4 v1 elaborates-predictor beats the bag-of-tokens baseline by only 6 pts and collapses on an unseen operator
  detail: >
    Run s4-verdict-20260911-152400 (sealed 40a2b7f7..., best val F1 0.936 @
    1500), held-out eval 3690e8f2... (calibration_failed): elaborates macro-F1
    0.941 (gate 0.80 PASS) but a bag-of-tokens logistic baseline reaches 0.880
    (+6.1 pts, gate >= 10 FAIL), and on the held-out operator swap_lit_01 the
    model scores 0.305 macro-F1 (near one-class). Operator identification 0.850.
    Reading: the Phase-3a `elaborates` label is largely LEXICALLY predictable for
    these operators (a `0` beside a `ℕ+`), so the task is too easy for a trivial
    baseline and the model learns operator-specific surface patterns rather than
    a general notion of well-typedness — exactly the distribution-shift failure
    the plan's OOD control exists to expose. Test n is small (326 IID / 65 OOD;
    5% module split of 8,187 statement mutants).
  fix: >
    (a) the substantive target is `gate_verdict` on statement mutants (does the
    mutated claim still hold?), which needs Phase-3b proof attempts (L-2) — a
    prover-in-the-loop stage; (b) a larger mutant sample (sample_mod 46 -> ~10)
    for a usable test set; (c) operators that are not lexically trivial
    (e.g. hypothesis WEAKENING/STRENGTHENING, quantifier swaps) so the baseline
    cannot shortcut. Until then, cite S4 only as "operator-conditional elaborates
    on this mutant distribution", never as a verdict predictor.
    2026-09-12 — S4 v2 on the Phase-3b label (fix a delivered): run
    s4v2-verdict-20260911-223439 on quod attempts mutants-A-r1 (6,152
    lock-verified rows, prover ladder-A, classes collapsed to proven vs
    not_proven because `rejected` has 0 support), eval metrics
    a9c99344... (calibration_failed): macro-F1 0.707 (gate 0.80 FAIL) but
    bag-of-tokens 0.526 → margin +18.1 pts (gate >= 10 PASS; the condition
    that gates the world model is cleared), permutation 0.49, OOD
    swap_lit_01 0.478. The failing number is macro-F1 over a `proven`
    class with 7 test rows (2.3% of the ladder's attempts succeed) — an
    under-powered gate, not evidence about the model. Fix (b) stands with a
    sharper form — more PROVEN rows (a stronger prover tier B, or every
    parent rather than sample_mod 46) — before the absolute gate means
    anything; val best 0.829 at step 2750.
