- id: L-7
  severity: high
  status: open
  title: tier-A transitions are terminal (closed/error), so the W0 next-state gate has no test set
  detail: >
    quod theorems-A-t1 (manifest 6fe9d067..., 5,410 sample_mod-46 theorems,
    ladder rfl/decide/simp/omega/exact?/aesop) emitted 26,130 transitions over
    5,923 goal states, but only 511 are state-to-state single steps (one goal
    in, a different goal out): 486 from simp, 23 from aesop; every other rung
    either closes the goal or errors. Module-held-out single steps: 12. The
    sealed W0 baselines (w0-baselines run, metrics fadd1511...) are therefore
    saturated (identity 0.917, offset 1.000, lexical 1.000 on 12 candidates;
    permutation 0.277) and cannot bound a learned predictor. Energy targets
    ARE well populated (5,408 states, closure rate over six rungs, energy mean
    1.16, 94 censored rows; table CID 19ba3da0...). Translation test: simp
    displacements have mean norm 2.48 with spread 1.97 — not a translation.
  fix: >
    The corpus needs a prover that STEPS. Options, in order of cost: (a) a
    stepping ladder (intro/constructor/ext/push_neg/norm_num/simp_all/
    ring_nf...) as a NEW recorded prover config with its own pilot and frozen
    P (ATTEMPTS.md decision 2 — a runner config change is a new prover, not
    a tweak; needs Nick's call); (b) tier B search (source=search) whose
    trajectories are multi-step by construction; (c) both, since the
    adversarial loop (item 8) needs a searcher anyway.
    2026-09-12 update — quod mutants-A-t1 (manifest 847bcbce...) changed the
    count: on MUTANTS, simp and aesop leave goals open instead of closing, so
    the wired corpus has 69,040 transitions, 7,331 single steps (simp 3,927,
    aesop 3,401), 284 module-held-out. Baselines re-sealed (metrics
    25c962b5...): identity 0.321, offset 0.792, lexical 0.579, permuted 0.021;
    energy table over 11,545 states, 880 censored. W0 v1 trained (w0-world-20260912-012400,
    val MRR best 0.843 @ 3250; eval 4e493983..., calibration_failed):
    next-state MRR 0.883 vs bar 0.895 (offset 0.795 + 0.10) — FAIL by 1.2
    pts; beats lexical by +30.4; permutation 0.025; per tactic aesop 0.846 vs
    offset 0.717 (+12.9, passes), simp 0.925 vs 0.871 (+5.5, does not);
    outcome-head macro-F1 0.596 over closed/error/open/budget (support
    245/2047/311/36); cost head MAE 0.76 in log-heartbeats on uncensored rows
    and predicts >= cap for only 11% of censored rows (mis-calibrated).
    Reading: the per-tactic mean offset is a strong predictor for simp
    (rewriting moves goals along a consistent direction); the learned model
    adds most on aesop. Caveat on the corpus itself: 93% of the single steps
    are on MUTANT goals (mostly false statements), so W0 v1 has learned the
    dynamics of failing searches. "JEPA" stays unused. Next: the stepping
    prover question above is unchanged (true-statement trajectories are
    still 511 rows); a calibrated cost head (weight the survival term, or a
    discrete cap-hit head) is a cheap second run.
